course: physics
lesson: 33
title: Levers: lifting heavy things with a little push
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A lever is a stiff bar that turns round a fixed point called a pivot, and it lets a small push do a big job.
explain: Push down on the long handle of a screwdriver jammed under a paint tin lid, and the short end near the rim pushes up so hard that the lid pops off.
explain: The secret is distance from the pivot: push far from it and you need only a little force, while close to it the bar pushes very hard but moves only a little.
explain: Levers can also swap the other way, giving up force for speed and reach, which is how your arm and a fishing rod flick things far and fast.
explain: Seesaws, scissors, wheelbarrows, bottle openers, nutcrackers, tongs and even your own arm are all levers.
fact: A lever is a stiff bar that turns about a pivot, and a small force far from the pivot makes a big force near it.
fact: The longer the handle of a lever, the easier the lift.
fact: A seesaw is a lever with the pivot in the middle, so a child sitting far out can lift a grown-up sitting close in.
fact: Archimedes, a Greek thinker, said that with a long enough lever and a place to stand he could move the Earth.
fact: Scissors are two levers joined at a pivot, so they cut thick card best close to the hinge.
fact: In a wheelbarrow the wheel is the pivot, and the load is easiest to lift when it sits close to the wheel.
fact: A nutcracker squeezes hardest on a nut placed close to the hinge.
fact: A crowbar with a small stone underneath as a pivot can lift a heavy stone slab; ancient builders moved giant stones with levers and ramps.
fact: Your arm is a lever with the elbow as the pivot; the muscle pulls close to the elbow, so it must pull hard, but the hand moves far and fast.
fact: Tongs and tweezers are levers pressed in the middle; they give less force but more control, so they suit careful work.
fact: A long lever like a fishing rod turns a small movement of the hand into a big, fast movement at the tip.
exercise: Why does a screwdriver help you lift the lid of a paint tin? | It is a lever, so a small push on the handle makes a big push on the lid | It is sharp, so it cuts the lid off | It is metal, so it pulls the lid like a magnet | It warms the lid so it expands
exercise: Why is a long screwdriver better than a short one for opening a tin? | The longer the handle, the easier the lift | A long one is heavier, so it presses down harder on the lid | A long one is sharper at the tip | A short one bends the lid inwards
exercise: How can a small child lift a grown-up on a seesaw? | By sitting far from the middle | By sitting right next to the middle | By jumping up and down hard | By leaning backwards on the seat
exercise: What did Archimedes say he could move with a long enough lever? | The Earth | The Moon | A mountain | The Sun
exercise: Where should you put thick card to cut it with scissors? | Deep in the blades, near the hinge | Right at the tips of the blades | Halfway along, it does not matter | Outside the blades, under the handle
exercise: In a wheelbarrow, what is the pivot? | The wheel | The handles | The load of soil | Your hands
exercise: Where should the heavy load sit in a wheelbarrow? | Close to the wheel | Close to the handles | Hanging over the back | It makes no difference at all
exercise: Why should you never open a bottle with your teeth? | You can break your teeth; a bottle opener lever does it safely | Teeth make the drink go flat | It spoils the taste of the drink | Teeth are too weak to touch metal
exercise: Why is a nut put close to the hinge of a nutcracker? | Near the pivot the lever squeezes hardest | Near the hinge the nut is held tight and cannot slip out | The far end is too narrow for a nut | The hinge is sharper than the handles
exercise: How could you lift a stone slab you cannot pick up? | Push down on a long bar resting on a small stone as a pivot | Tie a rope round it and pull straight up | Pour water on it to make it lighter | Hit it with a hammer until it rises
exercise: Is your arm a lever? | Yes, with the elbow as the pivot | No, only tools and machines can ever be levers | Yes, with the hand as the pivot | No, arms work like springs
exercise: What does your arm swap to move your hand fast? | Strength: the muscle pulls hard close to the elbow | Speed: the muscle moves slowly far from the elbow | Nothing, it gives strength and speed for free | Weight: the arm gets lighter as it moves
exercise: Why are tweezers good for pulling out a splinter? | They give less force but more control | They squeeze much harder than fingers | They are sharp enough to cut skin | They pull with a magnet
exercise: Why are fishing rods so long? | A small hand movement makes the tip move far and fast | Long rods catch bigger fish | The fish cannot see the person behind | Long rods are lighter than short ones
exercise: Which of these is NOT used as a lever? | A drinking glass | A bottle opener | A pair of scissors | A crowbar
test: mate says levers are magic, you get something for free. right? | No, you push less, but you have to push further | Yes, levers make energy out of nothing | Yes, a lever doubles your strength and your speed | No, levers only make things harder to lift
test: Your big brother sits on the seesaw and you can't lift him. What should you do? | Ask him to move closer to the middle | Sit closer to the middle yourself so you are nearer the pivot | Push down more gently | Take your shoes off
test: Leo tries to cut a thick cereal box with the tips of the scissors. What tip would you give him? | Slide the card deep in, near the hinge | Use the very tips of the blades and press much harder | Cut faster so it does not notice | Turn the scissors upside down
test: In a cartoon, a mouse lifts an elephant with a tiny short plank. What would really be needed? | A very long plank with the pivot near the elephant | A thicker plank with the pivot near the mouse | Nothing more, any plank will do | A plank made of steel instead
test: oi, stop loading all the bricks at the handle end of the wheelbarrow, you muppet. why? | The load is easier to lift close to the wheel | Bricks at the wheel end will make the tyre burst flat | The wheel will burst under bricks | The handles are made for bricks
test: How could ancient people move giant stones without machines? | With levers and ramps | They carried them on their heads | They melted and re-poured them | They were much stronger than us
test: Your hand is far from the elbow, but your muscle is fixed close to it. What does that mean? | The muscle pulls hard, and your hand moves fast | The muscle pulls gently, and your hand moves slowly | The muscle does not need to work at all | Your hand pushes the muscle along
test: Why would it be silly to lift a heavy stone with tweezers? | Tweezers give less force, not more | Tweezers are too long and too strong | Stones are magnetic and stick to them | Tweezers only work on wood
test: Dad gets a short screwdriver and a long one to open a stuck tin. Which should he pick? | The long one | The short one | Either, length does not matter | Neither, levers do not help with tins
test: What would happen if you put the walnut at the far end of the nutcracker handles? | It would be much harder to crack | It would crack much more easily | It would fly out and crack by itself | Nothing would change
test: Why can a tennis racket send the ball so fast? | It works like a long arm, turning small wrist moves into big fast ones | It is full of springs that push the ball | Its strings are magnetic | It is heavier than the ball
exam: Identify the fixed point about which a lever rotates. | The pivot | The load | The effort | The handle
exam: Ascertain the position in which a load ought to be placed in a wheelbarrow so as to minimise the effort required. | In close proximity to the wheel | In close proximity to the handles | Overhanging the rear of the barrow | The position is immaterial to the effort
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the operation of a lever employed to raise a heavy object? | A small force applied far from the pivot yields a greater force near it | A large force applied near the pivot yields a smaller force far from it | The lever creates additional energy for the user | The lever reduces the weight of the object itself
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the human forearm. | It constitutes a lever, the elbow serving as its pivot | It constitutes a pulley, the wrist serving as its wheel | It constitutes a screw, the shoulder serving as its thread | It operates by no mechanical principle whatsoever
